            <ArticleTitle>Management of chronic neuropathic pain following mandibular advancement surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1374-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijom.2012.04.018</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0901-5027(12)00175-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This report highlights the management of a rare complication, namely allodynia, which arose following mandibular advancement surgery. A 56-year-old female underwent bilateral sagittal ramus advancement osteotomy. Postoperatively, she developed allodynia corresponding to the distribution of the left inferior alveolar nerve. A hierarchical pathway of topical and pharmacological agents followed by ablative techniques was used without success. Eventually, electrical neuromodulation via a motor cortex stimulator was implemented to manage the symptoms. The report serves to outline treatment options available and the risks associated with this treatment.</AbstractText>
